# Paper Skeleton (one sentence per paragraph slot)

## Abstract
Whisper is a speech recognition system trained on 680,000 hours of weakly supervised multilingual audio that demonstrates substantially better zero-shot robustness than supervised baselines while approaching human-level accuracy on long-form transcription.

## 1. Introduction

**P1 (PROBLEM):** Speech recognition models trained on small curated datasets fail to generalize across acoustic conditions, domains, accents, and languages. {C014}

**P2 (KNOWN):** Prior work on self-supervised pre-training scales to large unlabeled corpora but requires task-specific fine-tuning for deployment; multi-dataset supervised training improves robustness but remains small in scale. {E030}

**P3 (GAP):** Large-scale weak supervision—using noisy internet audio-transcript pairs—has been underexplored as a path to zero-shot generalization. {C015}

**P4 (RQ + CONTRIBUTION):** This work asks whether training a single model on 680,000 hours of weakly supervised, multilingual and multitask audio produces a robustly generalizing speech recognizer; the result, Whisper, answers yes. {C001, C003}

**P5 (PAPER MAP):** The paper describes the dataset, architecture, and multitask training format; reports zero-shot results across English, multilingual, translation, noise, and long-form tasks; and analyzes scaling with model size and dataset size.

## 2. Method

**P6 (DATASET):** The training corpus of 680,000 hours spans 97 languages with English-heavy composition and includes transcription, translation, and language identification tasks. {C001, C014}

**P7 (DATA QUALITY):** Web-sourced transcripts are filtered using heuristics to reduce machine-generated captions and mislabeled audio; no explicit regularization is used, relying instead on dataset diversity. {E029}

**P8 (INPUT REPRESENTATION):** Audio is converted to 80-channel log-magnitude Mel spectrograms in 30-second segments, the standard frequency representation for speech models. {E026}

**P9 (ARCHITECTURE):** Whisper uses an encoder-decoder Transformer family (Tiny to Large, 39 M to 1.55 B parameters) with architecture details shown in Table 1. {E003}

**P10 (MULTITASK FORMAT):** Task-conditioning tokens prepended to the decoder specify language, task (transcription vs translation), and timestamp prediction, enabling a single model to handle all tasks. {E003}

**P11 (TRAINING):** All models are trained for 220,000 updates (2–3 passes over the dataset) using AdamW; no data augmentation or regularization is applied. {E025, E029}

**P12 (LONG-FORM INFERENCE):** For audio exceeding 30 seconds, sliding-window inference with beam search (5 beams), temperature fallback, VAD, and previous-text conditioning is applied. {E027}

## 3. Evaluation

**P13 (EVAL SETUP):** All results are zero-shot; WER evaluation uses a custom text normalization scheme developed alongside the model, with validation against the independent FairSpeech normalizer. {L001}

**P14 (ENGLISH WER LIBRISPEECH):** Whisper Large V2 achieves 2.7% WER on LibriSpeech test-clean. {C002}

**P15 (OUT-OF-DISTRIBUTION ENGLISH):** Across 12 out-of-distribution English datasets, Whisper Large V2 achieves 12.8% average WER, a 55.2% relative reduction from the 29.3% wav2vec 2.0 baseline. {C003}

**P16 (MULTILINGUAL):** Per-language WER on Fleurs shows strong log-linear correlation with training data volume (r²=0.83; WER halves per 16× data); Multilingual LibriSpeech results are competitive with but not universally best-in-class. {C004, E032}

**P17 (VOXPOPULI NEGATIVE):** On VoxPopuli, Whisper (13.6% WER) substantially underperforms mSLAM (9.1%) and XLS-R (10.6%), illustrating that zero-shot evaluation can be confounded by domain-specific pre-training of baselines. {C016}

**P18 (TRANSLATION):** Whisper achieves 29.1 BLEU on CoVoST2 X→en zero-shot; translation data-performance correlation (r²=0.24) is much weaker than for ASR, indicating noisier training signal. {C005, C018}

**P19 (LANGUAGE ID):** Language identification achieves 64.5% on all 102 Fleurs languages (80.3% on the 82 overlapping with training), underperforming supervised SOTA by 13.6 percentage points. {C020}

**P20 (NOISE ROBUSTNESS):** Under pub noise at SNR below 10 dB, Whisper outperforms all 14 LibriSpeech-trained baselines, a direct consequence of training on acoustically diverse data. {C006}

**P21 (LONG-FORM):** On 7 long-form datasets, Whisper outperforms NVIDIA STT on all; within 1.15% WER of computer-assisted human transcription on a 25-recording Kincaid46 subset. {C011, C012}

**P22 (DECODING ABLATION):** Incrementally adding beam search, temperature fallback, VAD, previous-text conditioning, and timestamp constraints reduces average long-form WER from 11.0% to 10.0%. {C017}

## 4. Scaling Analysis

**P23 (MODEL SCALING):** Performance improves monotonically from Tiny (39 M) to Large V2 (1.55 B) across all tasks. {C007}

**P24 (DATASET SCALING ENGLISH):** English WER follows rapid improvement from 3,400 to ~14,000 hours then slower gains approaching saturation; Table 2 summarizes the full trajectory. {C008}

**P25 (DATASET SCALING MULTILINGUAL + TRANSLATION):** Multilingual WER and X→en BLEU show steeper absolute gains over the same data range, with translation benefiting most from scale. {C009, C019}

**P26 (MULTITASK TRANSFER):** Joint multilingual/multitask training shows negative transfer at small model sizes but positive transfer at large scales, suggesting model capacity is the gating factor for cross-task benefits. {C010}

## 5. Discussion

**P27 (DATA BIAS LIMIT):** English-centric composition (65%) limits non-English performance; the strong data–WER correlation implies targeted low-resource data collection is the primary lever. {C014, C004}

**P28 (EVAL METHODOLOGY LIMIT):** Text normalization jointly developed with Whisper risks overfit to model-specific error patterns; the comparison is therefore WER under this specific normalization, not a universal metric. {L001}

**P29 (ZERO-SHOT ONLY LIMIT):** Fine-tuning on task-specific data was not studied; ceiling performance under adaptation is unknown. {E028 note}

**P30 (LONG-FORM HEURISTICS LIMIT):** Decoding heuristics for long-form transcription compensate for fundamental seq2seq instabilities; hallucination and repetition loops remain unresolved failure modes. {L009}

**P31 (ENCODER VS DECODER LIMIT):** It is unclear whether robustness arises from the encoder, the decoder, or both, because no ablation separating them was performed. {E022}

**P32 (OPEN QUESTIONS):** Future work includes fine-tuning studies, encoder/decoder ablations, improved data filtering for non-English languages, and characterization of ASR scaling laws. {E022}

## 6. Conclusion

**P33:** Whisper demonstrates that large-scale weak supervision produces a speech recognition system with substantially better out-of-distribution robustness than narrow supervised baselines, approaching human transcription accuracy on long-form English audio, with consistent gains from scaling both model size and dataset size.

